  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 325 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nPrecip/Storm Potential Tonight through Tuesday Night...The \nprevailing high pressure system will gradually depart tonight and \nallow return flow to increase over the area ahead of an approaching \nshortwave/trough and cold front. Ahead of the front, a southwesterly \nLLJ (35-45 kts) and increasing WAA will aid in rain showers \ndeveloping over eastern MN and western WI late tonight. These \nshowers will move into central and north-central WI by early Tuesday \nmorning and gradually move east/southeastward across the remainder \nof the forecast area through the rest of the morning. Cannot rule \nout the potential of some elevated thunderstorms producing a few \nstrong storms capable of strong wind gusts or hail given 400-800 \nJ/kg of MUCAPE and effective shear around 30 kts. This shower/storm \nactivity will lessen in coverage by Tuesday afternoon as the \nLLJ/instability decreases, but will not end. Some CAMs show an \nuptick in the shower/storm activity during the evening and night \nwith the arrival of the cold front, but there is plenty of \nuncertainty in this development. If these storms develop, a few \nstorms could be strong/severe with 400-800 J/kg of MUCAPE (including \nsome 100-200 J/kg of SBCAPE) and 30-40 kts of deep-layer shear. \nThere will also be an increased risk of heavy rainfall/downpours \ngiven PWATs will be around 1.75-2.00 inches throughout the period. \nIn addition to the precip/storm potential, breezy south winds are \nexpected on Tuesday gusting to 20-25 mph.\n\nRest of the Forecast...Dry conditions are forecast for Wednesday \nthrough much of Friday as high pressure prevails. However, following \nthe cold frontal passage Tuesday night, a tight pressure gradient \nwill develop on Wednesday leading to gusty westerly winds of 30-35 \nmph. The next chance of showers and storms arrives Friday night into \nSaturday with a cold frontal passage. Confidence is low on timing \nand placement of convection. Aside from near normal temperatures on \nThursday, but temperatures will remain above normal.",
